digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56787-w
  rankdir="TB";
  "I1" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Env affects pathogen, and if env oscillates then colonization is time-specific, so time-specific infection occurs."];
  "I19" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Clock is critical context, and mechanism elusive, so strong need to find clock targets."];
  "I3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Infection alters clock genes, and mechanism is elusive, so need to find specific targets."];
  "I5" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Genomes available, and clock defence reported in Hpa, so Hpa is a good model system."];
  "I7" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: RXLR structure known, and functions uncharacterized, so they are potential unknown manipulators."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates whether the Hyaloperonospora arabidopsidis RXLR effector HaRxL10 promotes pathogen virulence in Arabidopsis by binding to and stabilizing the circadian regulator CCA1 HIKING EXPEDITION, disrupting CHE transcriptional activity, reprogramming core clock gene expression, and suppressing salicylic-acid-mediated plant immunity.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence that a pathogen effector candidate interacts with a plant circadian regulator, alters its transcription-related behavior, and affects clock/immunity outputs motivates a focused mechanistic study of effector-mediated clock manipulation."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Given the suitability of the Hpa-Arabidopsis system and the plausibility of RXLR-motif cytoplasmic effectors as Hpa defence-manipulation candidates, focusing on Hpa RXLR effectors is justified."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The need to identify pathogen targets in the plant clock and the justified focus on Hpa RXLR effectors support the hypothesis that Hpa RXLR effectors may target clock components."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Because clock targets need to be identified and RXLR effector functions remain largely uncharacterized, Hpa RXLR effectors are reasonable candidates to test for interactions with clock components."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) A Y2H screen between Hpa effectors and Arabidopsis clock components directly tests whether Hpa RXLR effectors interact with clock components."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) In the Y2H screen testing Hpa effector interactions with clock components, HaRxL10 interaction with CHE identifies HaRxL10 as a positive candidate hit."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) CHE combines TCP-family transcription-factor properties, including recognition of TBS cis-elements, with an established role as a central clock component that represses CCA1."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) CHE links clock-component interactions with salicylic-acid-mediated plant immunity, based on its interactions with TOC1/ZTL and its reported role in SA-mediated immunity."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) CHE is a relevant candidate interface between transcriptional clock regulation and salicylic-acid-mediated plant immunity."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because plant immunity is coupled to the circadian clock and pathogen interference with plant circadian rhythms is conceivable, a pathogen-clock link is a plausible hypothesis to investigate rather than an established fact."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) HaRxL10 interacts with CHE in ways that can stabilize CHE by interfering with ZTL-mediated degradation while also repressing CHE binding to downstream genes."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) HaRxL10 is associated with reprogramming of clock-gene transcriptomes and with CHE-dependent effects on immunity and physiology."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Time-specific infection and a plausible pathogen-clock link make the circadian clock a relevant context for investigating plant-pathogen interactions."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because circadian timing is relevant to infection and pathogen-triggered clock-gene perturbations have elusive targets, there is a strong motivation to identify specific pathogen targets in the plant clock."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Hpa is an oomycete downy mildew pathogen that, as an obligate biotroph, can grow only on living plants."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The Hpa-Arabidopsis system is a suitable model for studying circadian-mediated plant defence because Hpa is an obligate biotrophic pathogen and genomic and prior clock-defence resources exist for Hpa-Arabidopsis studies."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) RXLR-motif cytoplasmic effectors are plausible Hpa effector candidates for studying how Hpa may counteract plant defences."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently there are genome sequences available for both Hpa and Arabidopsis."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Generally, circadian-mediated plant defence response was first reported in Hpa-Arabidopsis studies."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) If Hpa counteracts defences, then it secretes apoplastic or cytoplastic effectors."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently there is a subset of cytoplastic effectors characterized by the RXLR motif."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) Known that RXLR-type effectors contain signal peptides, motifs, and effector domains."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) Observed that bioinformatic analysis predicted 130 RXLR effectors but functions are uncharacterized."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently there is a Y2H screen testing interaction between 50 Hpa effectors and 11 clock components."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Observed that effector HaRxL10 interacted with clock components including CHE in yeast."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) Known that CHE belongs to TCP family transcription factors and recognizes TBS cis-elements."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently there is evidence CHE is a central clock component repressing CCA1."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Observed that pathogen life cycles are strongly affected by environmental factors like temperature and humidity."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently there is evidence CHE interacts with other clock components like TOC1 and ZTL."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) Generally, CHE plays a crucial role in SA-mediated plant immunity."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Observed that HaRxL10 interacted with CHE to interfere with ZTL-mediated CHE degradation."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Observed that while HaRxL10 stabilised CHE, it repressed CHE's binding to downstream genes."];
  "S24" [label="(24,0,0) Observed that HaRxL10 triggered reprogramming of transcriptome affecting clock genes."];
  "S25" [label="(25,0,0) Observed that HaRxL10 acted as a virulent effector impacting immunity and physiology in CHE-dependent manner."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) If environmental conditions oscillate daily, then pathogen colonization success happens at specific times."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Known that plants couple their immune system with the circadian clock to anticipate infections."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Observed phenomenon that it is conceivable pathogens may interfere with plant circadian clock."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Observed that pathogen infection can alter the expression pattern of plant circadian clock genes."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Known that how phytopathogens trigger these perturbations and specific targets remain elusive."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently there is a downy mildew pathogen Hpa which belongs to oomycetes."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) Generally, as an obligate biotroph, Hpa can only grow on living plants."];
  "S2" -> "I1" [label="deduction-case"];
  "S3" -> "I1" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "S6" -> "I3" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
